amiagent 0.13.2 - the Amiga half of amimcp
==========================================

New in 0.13.2:
  * Shell commands now fail at once on a missing volume instead of waiting
    on a requester. BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the agent sets pr_WindowPtr to -1 for
    itself, and the process that runs your commands inherits it. So a
    command naming a volume that is not mounted (Dir FOO:, Copy DF1:x RAM:)
    gets a DOS error - "object not found" or similar - instead of an
    "insert volume" requester waiting for someone at the machine. Before,
    only the TLS build did this; a plain build could put up that requester
    from LIST, GET, PUT or SHOT and stall the whole agent (ARexx QUIT
    included) until somebody clicked Cancel. Started from a Shell, the
    Shell's own setting is put back when the agent exits.
  * GETRANGE stops at the end of the file. A range running past the end
    now returns only the bytes that exist: a reply shorter than the length
    asked for means end of file, and an offset exactly at the end gets an
    empty reply. Agents before 0.13.2 announced the full length and then
    sent less, leaving the client waiting mid-reply; clients talking to an
    older agent should size the file with LIST first.
  * Screenshots of planar screens with fewer than 256 colours no longer
    carry noise in the high bits of each pixel (the temp bitmap was not
    cleared). The Mac side masks this for older agents too.
  * Mouse moves land where they were asked to on every screen mode. The
    pointer position was taken in the display's finest raster, so on a
    640x256 hires screen y=170 arrived as 85, and lores, SuperHires and
    dragged-down screens were off too. Warps are now scaled by the mode's
    resolution over the monitor's mouse ticks, plus the screen's offset;
    RTG screens, which were already right, stay 1:1.
  * The CPU is reported as 68080 on an Apollo/Vampire, not 68040.
  * The source builds a 68080-optimised agent with make CPU=68080 (not
    included in this archive; the 68020 build runs on an 080).
  * Thanks: xmies23 reported the requester, GETRANGE and screenshot bugs
    (#6, #5, #4) with the cause already found; mightym (Mark Wirblich)
    found and fixed the pointer scaling (PR #7); ZyberSE contributed the
    68080 build target and CPU detection (PR #3).

New in 0.13.1:
  * LIST now reports a soft link as type L instead of D. A soft link used to
    be reported as a directory whatever it pointed at, so a client walking
    "directories" (a file copier, say) would try to descend into a link to
    a file. Hard links are still shown as DOS presents them. Clients that
    only test for D see an L entry as a plain file, which is the safe reading.
    Found by amifleet68's copier, on the Amigo's RAM:Disk.info.

New in 0.13.0:
  * You can start it from Workbench now, and configure it there. Double-click
    amiagent and the settings come from the icon's TOOL TYPES - a Workbench
    start has no command line to put them on. Select the icon,
    Icons/Information, and edit:

        TOKEN=amiga4000   the shared secret. SET THIS ONE.
        PORT=7846         the port to listen on
        VERBOSE           open a window and log every request to it
        DONOTWAIT         only for a copy of this icon in SYS:WBStartup;
                          Workbench itself reads it, the agent does not

    The icon in this archive ships all four in parentheses -
    "(TOKEN=put-your-secret-here)" - which is the Workbench convention for
    "present but inactive". Take the parentheses off the ones you want.

  * Started from an icon there is no console to print to, so the two things
    you would otherwise never see are requesters instead: no TOKEN set
    (Start anyway / Quit), and a startup failure such as your TCP/IP stack
    not being up yet. With VERBOSE you get a window instead, and Ctrl-C in
    that window stops the agent.

  * NEW on the AMIAGENT ARexx port: QUIT. Without a console and without a
    CLI number there is nothing for Status/Break to find, so this is how a
    Workbench-started agent stops:

        address AMIAGENT
        'QUIT'

    Requested by djbase.

New in 0.12.0:
  * NEW on the AMIAGENT ARexx port: TASKPRI and TASKLIST.

        address AMIAGENT
        'TASKPRI "imp3" -1'     /* RESULT: imp3 pri 0 -> -1 */
        'TASKLIST "imp"'        /* RESULT: IMP3 - Debugger (0), imp3 (-1) */

    TASKPRI sets a task's priority BY NAME - the reach ChangeTaskPri lacks,
    which only addresses CLI process numbers while the tasks you actually
    need to re-prioritise (a Workbench-started player spinning at 100% CPU
    and starving its own audio decoder) have no CLI number at all. An exact
    name match beats a substring match, so "imp3" picks the player, not
    "IMP3 - Debugger". rc=0 + RESULT "name pri old -> new", rc=5 no such
    task. TASKLIST [pattern] lists matching tasks with their priorities.

  * FIX: a client-sent ARexx program (CMD_AREXX) that itself did
    `address AMIAGENT` deadlocked the agent - it waited for RexxMast's
    reply while RexxMast waited for the agent's own port. The agent now
    keeps serving its ARexx port while a client program runs. If you
    script the port from the network side, you want this version.

New in 0.11.0:
  * NEW: a local ARexx port, "AMIAGENT". Your own ARexx scripts and
    ARexx-aware applications can now drive the agent's input machinery
    directly, no network or AI involved:

        /* activate a window by title pattern, then type into it */
        address AMIAGENT
        'ACTIVATEWINDOW "CHAT --*"'      /* rc=0 found+activated, rc=5 not */
        'ENTERTEXT "hello there<enter>"'

    Commands: ACTIVATEWINDOW <pattern> (AmigaDOS wildcards, '*' works,
    plain text matches as substring; on rc=0 RESULT is the matched title),
    ENTERTEXT/TYPE <text> (typed through the active keymap; inline key
    tokens in the commodities style: <enter>, <esc>, <f1>..<f10>,
    <ctrl c>, <shift tab>, <lamiga q>, "<<" for a literal '<'),
    KEY <name>, VERSION, HELP. rc=0 means it worked; rc=5 window not
    found; rc=10 bad command. Check for the port with
    show('P','AMIAGENT') before addressing it, and it degrades cleanly:
    if another agent instance already owns the port, the second one
    simply runs without it.

  * PROTOCOL.md - the full wire-protocol description - now ships in this
    archive.

New in 0.10.0:
  * REXXPORTS: list the public message ports, so a client can discover
    what is scriptable right now.
  * GETRANGE: chunked download of any byte range of a file, for pulling
    big files without one giant frame.
  * The verbose log now timestamps every line.
  * Optional TLS via AmiSSL 5 (opt-in SSL=1 build; the agent self-signs
    a certificate on first run). The binaries in this archive are plain
    TCP - TLS needs a rebuild with the AmiSSL SDK.

New in 0.9.0:
  * CMD_AREXX: clients can run ARexx programs on this Amiga - address
    any application's port and get the RESULT back.
  * A semantic GUI layer: CMD_UITREE walks the Intuition windows and
    gadgets of the front screen as text, CMD_UIACT clicks/types/closes
    by object ("the OK button") instead of by pixel, CMD_MENUS
    enumerates menu strips and invokes items by shortcut.

New in 0.7.0:
  * NEW: amimon-mui, a MUI front-end for the status monitor. Same rows as
    amimon - agent, state, request, command, counters, client, driver,
    uptime - in a resizable MUI window that follows your MUI settings.
    Needs MUI 3.8+ (muimaster.library v19); if you do not have MUI, keep
    using amimon, which runs everywhere. Neither is the "lesser" monitor:
    they show exactly the same board and are maintained in lockstep.

  * The icons are now GlowIcons as well: each .info still carries the
    classic 4-colour planar images (what OS 2.x/3.1 shows), and after them
    an OS 3.5-style ColorIcon (FORM ICON) with a real transparent
    background and a glow on selection. icon.library v44+ - OS 3.5, 3.9,
    3.2, or PeterK's replacement - picks the colour version automatically;
    older systems never see it. amimon finally gets the icon it was always
    meant to have (amimon.info, a monitor showing a readout).

  * The agent's own code is unchanged apart from the version string; the
    wire protocol is untouched.

New in 0.6.1:
  * NEW COMPANION: amimon, a small status window you run on the Amiga
    itself. It watches amiagent through a named public semaphore (no
    network, no configuration) and shows, twice a second: whether a token
    is required, the request in flight, the last command, how many requests
    have been answered and failed, the client's address, and uptime. Start
    it any time from the drawer or a Shell - the fields fill in if the agent
    is running and say so if it is not. It carries its own icon (amimon.info).

  * The window also shows a "Driver" line: a self-reported label the client
    may announce (the new CMD_HELLO). It is exactly that - a claim the Amiga
    cannot verify - so it sits under the client's real IP, which is the part
    the machine actually knows. Handy for "which tool is poking my Amiga
    right now"; not a security fact.

  * No change to how existing commands behave, and the status board is
    local-only, so the wire protocol between Mac and Amiga is unchanged
    except for the optional, ignorable CMD_HELLO.

New in 0.5.5:
  * amiagent.info is now the APPLICATION icon, and belongs INSIDE the
    drawer next to the binary.

    0.5.4 shipped this artwork as the drawer icon, so the folder looked
    like an application instead of a folder.

  * The drawer icon and the icons for README.txt / license are deliberately
    NOT shipped. Your installer should make those from the system defaults
    (ENVARC:Sys/def_drawer.info and def_ascii.info) - then a folder looks
    like every other folder on your machine, matches whatever icon set you
    use, and gets the transparency the OS icons have. Shipping a hand-drawn
    drawer icon can do none of those things: a classic .info has no mask,
    so its background is a grey box on a patterned Workbench.

    Installing by hand:
        copy amiagent.info  to  SYS:Programs/amiagent/amiagent.info
        copy ENVARC:Sys/def_drawer.info to SYS:Programs/amiagent.info
        copy ENVARC:Sys/def_ascii.info  to SYS:Programs/amiagent/README.txt.info

  * Still no code change: the binaries are identical to 0.5.3.

New in 0.5.4:
  * Ships a drawer icon (amiagent.info), so the drawer is visible in
    Workbench instead of being there but invisible. It carries no saved
    position deliberately - your installer or Workbench decides where it
    sits, rather than the package insisting on a spot that may already be
    taken on your machine.
  * No code change: the binaries are identical to 0.5.3.

New in 0.5.3:
  * Reading a file now uses a 64 KB buffer instead of 8 KB, so a transfer
    makes an eighth as many round-trips to the filesystem. Pulling a big
    file off a real Amiga is noticeably quicker.
  * On a machine too tight on memory to spare the buffer it falls back to
    the old 8 KB path, so nothing changes on a small Amiga.

New in 0.5.2 (important - a second crash that could take the machine down):
  * Commands now run with a 256 KB stack instead of 16 KB. A command
    inherits the agent's stack, and a GUI program given too little does
    not fail politely on a 68k - it walks off the end of the stack and
    takes the machine with it.
  * ScummVM launched through the agent crash-rebooted an A4000 again and
    again, while the same binary started fine from Workbench - because
    an icon specifies its own stack and the agent did not. Running it
    with --version always worked, which is the tell: it exits before the
    call depth grows.
  * If you use amimcp to launch anything with a GUI, upgrade.

New in 0.5.1 (important - fixes a crash that could take the machine down):
  * The screen is now held while it is being captured. A screen pointer
    was being taken under Forbid(), released, and then used - and if that
    screen closed in the gap, the agent read freed memory. On AmigaOS
    that does not fail politely; it takes the machine with it.
  * The window is exactly when a program is starting up or dying, which
    is when you are most likely to be grabbing the screen to find out
    what went wrong. This crash-rebooted an A4000 while ScummVM was
    opening its screen.
  * Older than 0.5.0, but 0.5.0 is what made it easy to reach: region
    capture and hashing are cheap, so polling got aggressive, so the
    race got hit. If you run 0.5.0, upgrade.
  * A screen that closes mid-capture is now reported as an error.

New in 0.5.0:
  * Region capture. A screenshot can now ask for just a rectangle, and
    for a screen other than the frontmost one. Reading a small status
    line out of a 1080p truecolour frame used to move about a thousand
    times more data than the question needed - on an A4000 that is 7.4
    seconds versus effectively none.
  * Region checksums, for "has anything changed yet?" without moving any
    pixels at all. The right way to wait for a program to finish drawing.
  * Screen listing and pointer read-back, so a client can work out its
    coordinate mapping from real geometry instead of guessing - and can
    still see a healthy Workbench when a crashed program leaves a blank
    screen in front of it.
  * Input scripts: a whole sequence of events runs in one request with
    tick-accurate gaps. A press and a release sent as two requests land
    hundreds of milliseconds apart, which programs read as a held button
    rather than a click. Drags and double-clicks need real timing.

New in 0.4.0:
  * Relative mouse motion (RMOVE/HOME). Absolute pointer warps move the
    Intuition pointer, which Intuition programs follow - but anything
    reading raw mouse deltas (SDL, so ScummVM and games) never sees the
    warp and keeps its own cursor. A warp-then-click there silently
    clicks whatever that program still thought was under the pointer.
  * Full keystrokes. Key injection used to send press without release,
    which fails quietly: the Amiga simply behaves as though a finger is
    resting on the key. One unreleased Escape can lock a program out of
    accepting mouse input entirely.

New in 0.3.3:
  * Per-command output is now OFF by default. An AmigaShell pauses its
    output when you click in the window, and a process writing to a
    paused console blocks - which could hang the whole agent. Pass
    VERBOSE if you want the old chatter while debugging.
  * QUIET is still accepted; it just does nothing now.

New in 0.3.0:
  * A command that hangs no longer wedges the agent. Commands run in a
    child process; the agent stops waiting at the deadline and goes back
    to accepting connections. Screenshots, file transfer and input all
    keep working while a command is stuck.
  * New BREAK command sends Ctrl-C to a stuck command remotely, so you
    no longer have to walk over to the Amiga.

New in 0.2.0:
  * Mouse and keyboard injection - Claude can move the pointer, click,
    and type. Text goes through YOUR keymap, so umlauts come out right.
  * Native truecolor/RTG screen capture via cybergraphics.library, so
    screenshots no longer depend on C:sgrab (that still works as a
    fallback and produces smaller files).

0.1.1 fixed: commands returning their output, stderr capture on OS 3.2+,
and a token-less agent rejecting a client that offered one.

Lets Claude (running on the Mac) work on this Amiga: run AmigaDOS commands,
read and write files, list drawers, report system state, and grab the screen.

FILES
  amiagent         68000 build - runs on any Amiga, including this A4000
  amiagent.020     68020+ build - slightly smaller and faster, fine on an A4000
  amiagent.info    its Workbench icon (classic + GlowIcon in one file)
  amimon           68000 build of the status window (GadTools, runs everywhere)
  amimon.020       68020+ build of the status window
  amimon.info      its Workbench icon (a monitor showing a readout)
  amimon-mui       68000 build of the MUI status window (needs MUI 3.8+)
  amimon-mui.020   68020+ build of the MUI status window
  amimon-mui.info  its Workbench icon

NOTE: the token is case-sensitive and compared exactly. TOKEN=A4000 and
TOKEN=a4000 are different tokens.

TO QUIT
  Press Ctrl-C in the Shell window where amiagent is running.
  If you started it in the background with "run >NIL:", there is no window
  to type into - find it with C:Status and then break it, e.g.:

      Status                  (look for "Loaded as command: amiagent")
      Break 1 C               (use the process number Status printed)

  If you started it from its ICON there is no process number either. Use the
  ARexx port:

      address AMIAGENT
      'QUIT'

  - or, if you gave the icon a VERBOSE tool type, press Ctrl-C in the window
  it opened.

TO RUN
  1. Make sure your TCP/IP stack is up (Roadshow / AmiTCP / etc).
  2. From a Shell, in this drawer:

         amiagent TOKEN=amiga4000

     (If 0.1.0 is still running, press Ctrl-C in its window first.)

     It will print that it is listening on port 7846 and wait.
     Press Ctrl-C to stop it.

  3. Tell Claude the Amiga's IP address. If you don't know it, Roadshow
     users can find it with:

         C:ShowNetStatus              (or: AddNetInterface, then Ifconfig)

OPTIONS (Shell)
  PORT/N    listen on a different port     (default 7846)
  TOKEN/K   shared secret the Mac must send
  VERBOSE/S print each command as it runs (off by default)
  QUIET/S   accepted, ignored - quiet is the default now

TOOL TYPES (Workbench - Icons/Information on the amiagent icon)
  TOKEN=... shared secret the Mac must send. Set this one.
  PORT=...  listen on a different port     (default 7846)
  VERBOSE   open a window and log every request to it
  DONOTWAIT read by Workbench, not by the agent; put it on a copy of the icon
            in SYS:WBStartup so Workbench does not wait for the agent to exit

  Tool types and Shell arguments never meet: one start is one or the other.

TO START IT AT BOOT
  Add this to S:User-Startup, AFTER your TCP/IP stack comes up:

      run >NIL: <path-to>/amiagent TOKEN=amiga4000 QUIET

SECURITY
  amiagent runs whatever commands it is sent, and the connection is not
  encrypted. Always give it a TOKEN, keep it on your own LAN, and never
  forward the port through your router. Without a TOKEN, anything on the
  network can run any command on this machine.

TROUBLESHOOTING
  "no bsdsocket.library"     - your TCP/IP stack isn't running yet
  "cannot bind port 7846"    - amiagent is already running
  icon start does nothing    - it is running, quietly; that is what a
                               Workbench start looks like without VERBOSE.
                               amimon will show it.
  screenshots fail           - needs OS 3.0+ and a 256-colour-or-fewer screen
